Name: llama.cpp
Short Name: llama_cpp
URL: https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp
Version: b11641
Revision: 843e7dcc15098dd6b3aaf33b4847052293d8ef1a
Update Mechanism: Manual
License: MIT
License File: LICENSE
Security Critical: yes
Shipped: yes

Description:
LLM inference in C/C++ (llama.cpp and the ggml tensor library), used for
MoltBrowser's on-device model runtime. It parses untrusted model files
(GGUF) and prompt text, hence Security Critical. It runs only in a
sandboxed utility process: the inference service in
//chrome/services/molt_ai/inference (kOnDeviceModelExecution sandbox,
docs/design/SANDBOXED_INFERENCE.md), the only code BUILD.gn lets link it.
Built by BUILD.gn with the CPU backend everywhere and the Metal backend on
macOS (GGML_VERSION 0.26.0, LLAMA_VERSION 0.6.0-dev). With the GN switch
molt_llama_vulkan (llama_cpp.gni, off by default; Linux and Windows) the
Vulkan backend is built too, its SPIR-V compiled at build time with the
glslang and SPIRV-Tools Chromium pins
(docs/design/SANDBOXED_INFERENCE.md section 11). Image input uses the
multimodal library (tools/mtmd: the vision projector, image preprocessing
and the projectors' graphs), built as :mtmd; the browser decodes images
with Chromium's sandboxed decoders and the service receives raw RGB pixels
(docs/design/SANDBOXED_INFERENCE.md section 10).

Only the directories the build uses were vendored: common/, ggml/,
include/ and src/ from the tag above, and from tools/mtmd only the
library's own sources (clip*, mtmd.*, mtmd-internal.h, mtmd-image.*,
mtmd-audio.*, debug/mtmd-debug.h and models/), as listed in its
CMakeLists.txt's add_library(mtmd ...) minus mtmd-helper*.cpp. Not
vendored: mtmd-helper (it decodes compressed images with stb_image and
audio with miniaudio, and runs ffmpeg for video), the CLI, tests, test
media, conversion scripts, examples/ and vendor/.

Exceptions: BUILD.gn builds ggml_cpp, llama_models, llama_cpp,
llama_common and mtmd with exceptions; everything else, including the ggml-cpu
and ggml-metal sources and all MoltBrowser callers, is built with
Chromium's -fno-exceptions. So a `throw` or `.at(` miss in ggml-cpu or
ggml-metal aborts (none in the files BUILD.gn builds at b11641; only
the unbuilt ggml-cpu/spacemit/ has some), and an exception that
escapes a llama_* C function into a caller terminates the inference
process (the browser then reports the model as stopped; repeated
crashes are rate-limited). Model loading and context creation catch
everything upstream; see the llama-vocab.cpp patch below for
tokenization, and the list of checked calls at the top of
chrome/services/molt_ai/inference/llama_engine.cc. Constrained decoding
uses the grammar sampler (src/llama-grammar.cpp) unpatched: its parser
catches its own throws, and the accept path's throw and aborts are made
unreachable by the caller (chrome/services/molt_ai/inference/
grammar_sampler.cc: token bans, strict UTF-8 across pieces, only checked
tokens accepted). Re-check that file when updating. The lazy /
trigger-pattern grammars (std::regex on model output) are not used, and
common/json-schema-to-grammar.cpp is replaced by MoltBrowser's own
converter (chrome/services/molt_ai/inference/engine/
json_schema_grammar.cc). The mtmd_* entry points the service calls catch
their own exceptions (mtmd_init_from_file_ptr, mtmd_tokenize_from_parts,
mtmd_encode_chunk) or don't throw; their aborts are listed with each call
at the top of llama_engine.cc. Re-check them when updating.
ggml-vulkan (molt_llama_vulkan only) is built with exceptions: it uses
vulkan.hpp's throwing API, and on a lost device or a Vulkan error it
throws out of graph compute, synchronization and buffer clears, which
llama_decode, llama_process, llama_get_logits_ith, llama_sampler_sample
and llama_memory_clear don't catch. The service makes those calls through
chromium/llama_guard.{h,cc} (built with exceptions; catches everything),
on the CPU too. ggml_backend_vk_reg() catches its own errors. Re-check
both when updating.

Local Modifications:
- BUILD.gn: GN build for Chromium (not upstream). Every target that
  compiles llama.cpp or ggml code is visible only to
  //chrome/services/molt_ai/inference (the sandboxed inference service),
  :mtmd included.
- chromium/ggml-version.h, chromium/llama-version.h: stand-ins for the
  headers upstream CMake generates from ggml/src/ggml-version.h.in and
  src/llama-version.h.in. Update their values with every update.
- llama_cpp.gni and the molt_llama_vulkan block of BUILD.gn (not
  upstream): ggml-vulkan as a source_set, its shaders generated at build
  time. MoltBrowser files in chromium/ for it:
    ggml_vk_glslc.cc: a host tool implementing the subset of glslc's
      command line that vulkan-shaders-gen runs (Chromium has no shaderc),
      on Chromium's glslang and SPIRV-Tools: -O is spirv-opt's performance
      passes, as in glslc.
    ggml_vulkan_checks.py: build-time checks that every shader extension
      BUILD.gn enables still compiles with Chromium's glslang (upstream's
      CMake probes this at configure time), and that BUILD.gn's .comp list
      matches vulkan-shaders/ (update the list with every update).
    ggml-vulkan-moltbrowser.{h,cpp}: opens the Vulkan loader at run time
      (dlopen libvulkan.so.1 / LoadLibrary vulkan-1.dll) and reads the
      device properties the service's device policy needs.
  vulkan-shaders-gen.cpp is unmodified. The backend is not registered with
  ggml (no GGML_USE_VULKAN, so ggml-backend-reg.cpp doesn't bring it up);
  the service passes the chosen device explicitly.
- chromium/llama_guard.{h,cc} (not upstream): the exception boundary for the
  service's llama.cpp calls that can reach a GPU (see Exceptions above), and
  the bring-up of the Vulkan backend for it.
- Removed backends no BUILD.gn file references: ggml/src/ggml-cuda,
  ggml-hip and ggml-musa (both build ggml-cuda's sources), ggml-sycl,
  ggml-opencl, ggml-hexagon, ggml-webgpu, ggml-virtgpu, ggml-cann,
  ggml-zdnn, ggml-zendnn, ggml-et and ggml-openvino. ggml-blas and
  ggml-rpc are kept for possible future use; ggml-cpu and ggml-metal are
  built, and ggml-vulkan with molt_llama_vulkan. ggml/src/CMakeLists.txt
  and ggml/src/ggml-vulkan/CMakeLists.txt still list them and are not used
  by the build.
- Patched against the tag (diff these against upstream b11641 when
  updating; every patch is marked "MoltBrowser" in the source except the
  added #includes):
    ggml/src/ggml-backend-dl.h: #include <memory>.
    ggml/src/ggml-backend-reg.cpp: #include <cstdlib>.
    ggml/src/ggml-metal/ggml-metal-ops.cpp: #include <utility> (std::swap
      with Chromium's C++ modules).
    ggml/src/ggml-metal/ggml-metal.cpp: #include <memory>.
    ggml/src/ggml-vulkan/ggml-vulkan.cpp,
    ggml/src/ggml-vulkan/ggml-vulkan-buffers.cpp: ggml-vulkan is built with
      VK_NO_PROTOTYPES, so it can't reference a Vulkan entry point at link
      time (upstream links the loader, which would make the whole browser
      binary need libvulkan.so.1 / import vulkan-1.dll into every process).
      With GGML_VULKAN_MOLTBROWSER the dispatcher is initialized from
      ggml_vk_moltbrowser_get_instance_proc_addr() (chromium/
      ggml-vulkan-moltbrowser.cpp) instead of the global
      vkGetInstanceProcAddr, and the direct calls of vkGetInstanceProcAddr,
      vkGetDeviceProcAddr, vkGetPhysicalDeviceFeatures2 (three) and
      vkCmdCopyBuffer (two, in -buffers.cpp) go through
      VULKAN_HPP_DEFAULT_DISPATCHER (equivalent upstream too). A new direct
      call fails to compile, which is the point.
    ggml/src/ggml.c, ggml/src/ggml.cpp: with GGML_MOLTBROWSER_NO_BACKTRACE
      (set by BUILD.gn's :ggml_no_backtrace), ggml_print_backtrace() does
      nothing and no std::set_terminate handler is installed. Upstream
      forks the browser process on GGML_ABORT, allows the child to ptrace
      it (PR_SET_PTRACER) and execs gdb/lldb found on PATH; the terminate
      handler is a static initializer replacing Chromium's own.
    src/llama-adapter.cpp: #include <iterator>.
    src/llama-chat.cpp: llm_chat_template_from_str() uses find() instead
      of map::at(), so a template that is not a bare name is not
      signalled with std::out_of_range.
    src/llama-chat.cpp, src/llama-chat.h: built-in Gemma 4 chat format
      (LLM_CHAT_TEMPLATE_GEMMA4, "gemma4"; text only, thinking off).
      Upstream formats Gemma 4 only through common/'s Jinja engine, which
      is not built. Matches upstream's Jinja rendering byte for byte for
      the Gemma 4 templates in upstream's models/ directory.
    src/llama-vocab.cpp: llama_tokenize(), llama_token_to_piece() and
      llama_detokenize() catch std::exception and return an error
      (INT32_MIN, 0, INT32_MIN) instead of letting it escape into
      -fno-exceptions callers.
    src/llama.cpp, src/llama-quant.cpp: static_cast instead of
      dynamic_cast<llama_model_base *> (Chromium builds with -fno-rtti;
      every model type derives from llama_model_base).
    src/llama-mmap.cpp: with LLAMA_MOLTBROWSER_SANDBOXED (set on
      :llama_cpp by BUILD.gn's :llama_sandboxed), the Linux mmap of a
      model file doesn't pass MAP_POPULATE. The model is loaded in a
      sandboxed utility process, and Chrome's seccomp-bpf policy answers
      that flag with SIGSYS (RestrictMmapFlags in sandbox/linux/
      seccomp-bpf-helpers/syscall_parameters_restrictions.cc). The
      posix_madvise(POSIX_MADV_WILLNEED) that follows still prefetches
      the same range.
    src/llama-memory-hybrid-idx.cpp: struct kpool_layout defined before
      the constructor (as C++23, which Chromium uses, libc++ rejects the
      constexpr ~unique_ptr of an incomplete type there).
    tools/mtmd/clip.cpp, tools/mtmd/clip.h: clip_init_from_file_ptr()
      loads the projector from a FILE* the caller opened (the sandboxed
      service can't open paths): gguf_init_from_file_ptr() for the
      metadata, then every tensor read (read_tensor_data(), also used by
      get_vector()) seeks and reads that stream, and a short read throws
      (upstream ignores a short ifstream read). The path-based clip_init()
      reads as before.
    tools/mtmd/clip.cpp: with LLAMA_MOLTBROWSER_SANDBOXED (:llama_sandboxed
      on :mtmd), the MTMD_DEBUG_EMBEDDINGS environment variable is ignored:
      no dump of image embeddings to the log or to a file it names.
    tools/mtmd/mtmd.cpp, tools/mtmd/mtmd.h: mtmd_init_from_file_ptr(), the
      FILE* counterpart of mtmd_init_from_file() (catches everything and
      returns nullptr, like it).
    tools/mtmd/mtmd.cpp: mtmd_context's constructor frees the clip
      contexts when it throws after loading them (a projector made for
      another model's embedding width); upstream leaks them, as the
      destructor doesn't run.
- Patches dropped at b11641 because upstream now has them:
  src/llama-graph.h (#include <cstdlib>), src/llama-quant.cpp
  (#include <algorithm>).
